# 2. What's the difference between Monolith, Modular Monolith, and Microservices?

---

## A. Monolith

Everything exists inside one application.

```
HRMS

Employee

Department

Leave

Payroll

Attendance
```

Single project

Single deployment

Single application

---

### Advantages

Easy

Simple

Fast

Good for startups

Easy debugging

---

### Problems

As the project grows,

code becomes messy if not structured properly.

---

## B. Modular Monolith

Still ONE application

BUT

internally divided into modules.

```
Employee Module

Department Module

Payroll Module

Attendance Module

Leave Module
```

Each module owns:

* controller
* service
* repository
* entity
* dto

Modules communicate through clearly defined interfaces instead of becoming tightly coupled.

---

Advantages

* Clean organization
* Easier maintenance
* Easier testing
* Easier future migration
* Better ownership

---

## C. Microservices

Each module becomes its own application.

```
Employee Service

Department Service

Payroll Service

Attendance Service
```

Every service has

its own

* database
* deployment
* REST API

Services communicate over the network (typically HTTP or messaging).

---

Advantages

Independent scaling

Independent deployment

Independent teams

Fault isolation

---

Disadvantages

Very complex

Distributed transactions

Network failures

More DevOps work

Higher infrastructure cost

---

## Comparison

| Feature       | Monolith     | Modular Monolith             | Microservices             |
| ------------- | ------------ | ---------------------------- | ------------------------- |
| Deployment    | One          | One                          | Many                      |
| Database      | Usually one  | Usually one                  | Usually one per service   |
| Complexity    | Low          | Medium                       | High                      |
| Scalability   | Moderate     | Good                         | Excellent                 |
| Team Size     | Small        | Small–Medium                 | Medium–Large              |
| Communication | Method calls | Method calls between modules | Network calls             |
| Best For      | Small apps   | Growing enterprise apps      | Large distributed systems |

---

## Interview Answer

> A monolith is a single application where all features are packaged together. A modular monolith is still one application but organized into independent modules with clear boundaries, making it easier to maintain and evolve. Microservices split each business capability into independent deployable services that communicate over the network, offering greater scalability at the cost of additional operational complexity.

